Product Requirements Document (PRD)
Project: MIMAS Documentation Website (v2)
1. Purpose & Vision
1.1 Problem Statement

The current MIMAS website successfully documents the execution flow of the model, but it is:

tightly coupled to a single navigation pattern

difficult to extend beyond the “21-stage flow”

not optimized for learning, reference, and scientific explanation at multiple depths

visually minimal and developer-centric, rather than scientist-centric

As MIMAS grows into a thesis backbone, model reference, and potentially a public scientific resource, the website must evolve from a flow viewer into a structured scientific documentation platform.

1.2 Vision

Build a clean, modular, extensible scientific documentation website that:

explains what the model does, how it does it, and why

connects Fortran code, physics, and scientific literature

scales with future additions (new physics, experiments, plots)

can serve as:

your personal model notebook

a thesis companion

a handover document for future students

2. Target Users
Primary Users

You (Model Developer / Thesis Author)

Deep understanding

Needs traceability between code ↔ physics ↔ results

Uses site daily

Future MIMAS Users (PhD / MSc students)

Need onboarding

Need intuition + references

Often unfamiliar with Fortran

Secondary Users

Supervisors & Collaborators

Want high-level understanding

Want to verify assumptions

May skim, not read deeply

External Readers (optional future)

Interested in NLC modeling

Not expected to read code

3. Goals & Success Criteria
3.1 Core Goals

Make MIMAS understandable without reading Fortran

Preserve exact execution order and implementation fidelity

Separate code facts from scientific interpretation

Support incremental documentation (you don’t have to finish everything now)

3.2 Success Metrics

You can answer: “Where exactly is this physics implemented?” in <10 seconds

A new student can explain the full model flow after 1–2 days

You can link website sections directly in your thesis

Adding a new section does not require rewriting existing pages

4. Scope
In Scope

Static website (no backend)

Scientific documentation

Code explanation

Literature linkage

Visual structure for learning

Out of Scope (for now)

Running simulations

Uploading NetCDF files

Live plotting dashboards

User accounts

