6. Page-Level Requirements
6.1 Home Page

Purpose: Orientation + entry point

Requirements

One-sentence description of MIMAS

Clear distinction between:

What the model is

What the site contains

Entry buttons:

“Start with Overview”

“Jump to Model Flow”

Minimal cognitive load

6.2 Model Overview

Purpose: Conceptual understanding

Content

What MIMAS simulates

Why MIMAS exists

What is unique about it:

Eulerian H₂O + Lagrangian particles

Dust recycling

Fixed vs evolving dynamics

Key assumptions

Known limitations

Non-requirements

No code here

No equations (optional later)

6.3 Model Architecture

Purpose: Structural understanding

Requirements

Explain:

Grids (lat/lon/z)

Time stepping

Data sources (LIMA, climatologies)

Distinguish:

Background fields

Tracer fields

Particle ensemble

Diagram-first, text-second

6.4 Model Flow (Core Feature)

Purpose: Exact execution logic

This is the heart of the website.

Requirements

Preserve the 21-step execution logic

Each step must have three clearly separated layers:

What Happens

Procedural

No interpretation

Code Layer

Subroutines

Variables

Call relationships

Science Layer

Physical meaning

Why this step exists

What would break if removed

Strong Constraint

No scientific interpretation may contradict the code.

6.5 Physics Modules (New)

Purpose: Cross-cutting understanding

Structure
Each physics module is independent of execution order.

Example modules:

Water vapor transport

Eddy diffusion (Kz)

Photolysis & solar forcing

Ice microphysics

Sedimentation

Turbulence vs diffusion mismatch

Dust reallocation logic

Each module must answer

What is modeled

How it is implemented

Where it appears in the flow

Key references

6.6 Code Reference

Purpose: Fortran navigation without fear

Requirements

Alphabetical subroutine list

For each subroutine:

Purpose

Called by

Calls

Key variables

Snippets only (never full files)

Case-insensitive search

6.7 Experiments & Results (Future)

Purpose: Bridge to thesis

Planned Content

Experiment matrix

Parameter variations

Output variables

Key plots (static images)

Interpretation links

6.8 Institute & People

Purpose: Context and attribution

Requirements

Institute description

Department focus

Scientific motivation

Contributors (roles, not CVs)

6.9 Publications

Purpose: Scientific grounding

Requirements

Group papers by:

Core framework

Microphysics

Transport

Applications

Explicitly state:

What each paper contributes to MIMAS

7. Non-Functional Requirements
Performance

Load time < 1s on local server

No heavy JS frameworks

Maintainability

Content editable without touching JS

Clear separation:

data

layout

logic

Accessibility

Keyboard navigation

Readable without color

Longevity

Must still make sense 5 years later

No trendy UI dependencies

8. Design Principles

Scientist-first, not developer-first

Clarity beats cleverness

Explicit is better than implicit

Documentation is part of the model

9. Risks & Mitigations
Risk	Mitigation
Over-documentation	Incremental expansion
Mixing interpretation with fact	Strict layer separation
Thesis drift	Tie sections explicitly to chapters
Burnout	“Draft” is acceptable everywhere
